== Chapter 2

#align(center)[#smallcaps(all: true)[WHAT IT IS TO KNOW GOD,—THE AIM OF THIS KNOWLEDGE.]]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Sections.]]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[1\. The knowledge of God the Creator defined. The substance of this knowledge, and the use to be made of it.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[2\. Further illustration of its use, together with a necessary rebuke of vain curiosity, and a refutation of the Epicureans. The character of God as it appears to the pious mind, contrasted with the absurd views of the Epicureans. Religion defined.]

#v(0.8em)

1\. #smallcaps[By] the knowledge of God I mean the knowledge by which we not only conceive that there is some God, but also grasp what is in our interest and serves his glory—in short, what it is fitting to know about him. For, properly speaking, we cannot say that God is known where there is no religion or piety. I am not yet speaking of the kind of knowledge by which men who in themselves are lost and under the curse grasp God as Redeemer in Christ the Mediator. I speak only of that simple and original knowledge to which the genuine order of nature would have led us if Adam had remained upright. For although in the present ruin of the human race no man will now perceive God to be either a father, or the author of salvation, or favorable in any way, until Christ steps in to make our peace, still it is one thing to perceive that God our Maker upholds us by his power, rules us by his providence, cherishes us by his goodness, and attends us with every kind of blessing, and another thing to embrace the grace of reconciliation offered to us in Christ. Since, then, the Lord first appears simply as Creator, both in the making of the world and in the general doctrine of Scripture, and afterwards as Redeemer in the face of Christ, a twofold knowledge of him arises from this. The first of these is now to be considered; the second will follow later in its place. But although our mind cannot conceive of God without giving him some worship, it will not be enough simply to hold that he is the only being whom all ought to worship and adore, unless we are also persuaded that he is the fountain of all good, and that we must seek everything in him and in none but him. What I mean is this: we must be persuaded not only that, as he once made the world, so he sustains it by his boundless power, governs it by his wisdom, preserves it by his goodness, and above all rules the human race with justice and judgment, bears with it in mercy, and shields it by his protection; but also that not a particle of light, or wisdom, or justice, or power, or uprightness, or genuine truth will be found anywhere that does not flow from him and of which he is not the cause. In this way we must learn to expect and ask all things from him, and thankfully ascribe to him whatever we receive. For this sense of the divine perfections is the right master to teach us piety, out of which religion springs. By piety I mean that union of reverence and love for God which the knowledge of his benefits inspires. For until men feel that they owe everything to God, that they are cherished by his fatherly care, and that he is the author of all their blessings, so that nothing is to be sought apart from him, they will never submit to him in willing obedience; indeed, unless they place their entire happiness in him, they will never give up their whole selves to him in truth and sincerity.

2\. Those, therefore, who in considering this question set out to inquire what the essence of God is, only trifle with cold speculations, since it is far more in our interest to know what kind of being God is and what is in keeping with his nature. For what is the use of acknowledging, with Epicurus, some God who has thrown off the care of the world and only takes his pleasure in idleness? What good is it, in short, to know a God with whom we have nothing to do? Rather, our knowledge ought, #emph[first], to teach us reverence and fear; and, #emph[second], to lead us, under its guidance and teaching, to ask every good thing from him and, once it is received, to ascribe it to him. For how can the thought of God enter your mind without at once raising the thought that, since you are his workmanship, you are, by the very right of creation, made over and bound in subjection to his authority?—that you owe your life to him?—that whatever you do ought to be referred to him? If so, it surely follows that your life is badly corrupted if it is not shaped in obedience to him, since his will ought to be the law of our lives. On the other hand, your idea of his nature is not clear unless you acknowledge him to be the origin and fountain of all good. From this would arise both confidence in him and a desire to cling to him, if the depravity of the human mind did not lead it away from the right path of inquiry.

For, first of all, the pious mind does not dream up for itself just any kind of God, but looks only to the one true God; nor does it invent for him whatever character it pleases, but is content to have him as he reveals himself, always guarding with the utmost care against going beyond his will and straying, with daring rashness, from the right path. The man by whom God is known in this way, seeing that God governs all things, trusts him as his guardian and protector, and throws himself wholly on his faithfulness. Seeing that God is the source of every blessing, if he is in any difficulty or feels any lack, he at once takes refuge in God’s protection and looks to him for help. Persuaded that God is good and merciful, he rests on him with sure confidence, and does not doubt that in the divine clemency a remedy will be ready for his every need. Acknowledging God as his Father and his Lord, he considers himself bound to regard God’s authority in all things, to revere his majesty, to work for the advancement of his glory, and to obey his commands. Regarding him as a just judge, armed with severity to punish crimes, he keeps God’s judgment seat always before his eyes. In awe of it, he restrains himself and fears to provoke God’s anger. Yet he is not so terrified by the thought of judgment that he would wish to slip away, even if a way of escape lay open; rather, he embraces God no less as the avenger of wickedness than as the rewarder of the righteous, because he sees that it belongs equally to God’s glory to store up punishment for the one and eternal life for the other. Besides, it is not the fear of punishment alone that keeps him from sin. Loving and revering God as his father, honoring and obeying him as his master, he would shrink from the very thought of offending him, even if there were no hell.

This is pure and genuine religion: faith joined with a serious fear of God—a fear that both includes willing reverence and brings with it such lawful worship as the law prescribes. And it should be noted all the more carefully that all men without distinction pay homage to God, but very few truly revere him. Everywhere there is a great show of ceremonies, but sincerity of heart is rare.
